   "title": "The Herbalist's Companion",
   "author": "Mother Elsa",
   "about": "healing plants, poisons and how to keep herbs",
   "color": "#2d5b3a",
   "cover": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
   "spine": "data:image/jpeg;base64,",
   "chunks": [
    "Willowbark eases fever and aches. Strip the inner bark of a young white willow in spring, dry it in the shade, and steep a thumb's length of it in hot water for a quarter hour. Drink a cup at dawn and another at dusk. Too much upsets the stomach.",
    "Comfrey, which the old wives call knitbone, is bound over a broken limb as a poultice. Mash the fresh leaves with a little warm water, spread them on linen and wrap the limb. Change it every second day. It mends bones faster than rest alone, but never give it to drink.",
    "Nightshade grows in the damp shade behind the mill. Its berries are black and glossy and taste sweet, and a handful will stop the heart of a grown man. Children must be kept away from the patch. If someone has eaten them, make them vomit at once and send for the priest.",
    "Yarrow stops bleeding. Press the crushed leaves into a fresh cut and hold them there until the blood slows. Soldiers carry it dried in their pouches. A tea of the flowers also brings out a sweat and breaks a fever, though it is weaker than willowbark.",
    "Chamomile calms the nerves and brings sleep. Gather the small daisy-like flowers in high summer, dry them on a cloth, and steep a spoonful in hot water before bed. It is gentle enough for children with colic and old men who cannot sleep.",
    "Mandrake root is sold at fairs as a love charm, but it is a poison that brings visions and madness. The Herbalist's Guild forbids its sale. Any member found selling mandrake loses their licence and pays a fine of fifty crowns.",
    "On the keeping of herbs: store dried leaves in clay jars with tight lids, away from sunlight and damp. Roots keep for two years, leaves for one, and flowers lose their strength after a single winter. Label every jar with the plant and the month it was gathered.",
    "The road from Millbrook to Eastvale crosses the river at the old stone bridge. Travellers should carry bread for two days and a good cloak, for the hills are cold even in summer. The inn at the crossing charges three coppers a night."
   ],
